import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
 import pandas as pd
df = pd.DataFrame({
     "video_id": ["V01", "V02", "V03", "V04", "V05", "V06", "V07", "V08"],
     "sign": ["HELLO", "HELLO", "HELP", "HELP", "HELP", "YES", "YES", "NO"],
     "environment": ["indoor", "outdoor", "outdoor", "outdoor",
                     "indoor", "indoor", "indoor", "outdoor"],
     "quality": [5, 4, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5, 5]
 })
# Task 1 — Sign distribution
 df["sign"].value_counts().plot(kind="bar")
 plt.xlabel("Sign")
 plt.ylabel("Number of Videos")
 plt.title("Distribution of Videos per Sign")
 plt.show()
# Task 2 — Quality distribution
 df["quality"].plot(kind="hist", bins=5, edgecolor="black")
 plt.xlabel("Quality Score")
 plt.ylabel("Frequency")
 plt.title("Distribution of Video Quality Scores")
 plt.show()
# Task 3 — Average quality by sign
 df.groupby("sign")["quality"].mean().plot(kind="bar")
 plt.xlabel("Sign")
 plt.ylabel("Average Quality Score")
 plt.title("Average Video Quality Score by Sign")
 plt.show()
Here are the Python code scripts and research observations for Tasks 1 through 4:
 import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
 import pandas as pd
df = pd.DataFrame({
     "video_id": ["V01", "V02", "V03", "V04", "V05", "V06", "V07", "V08"],
     "sign": ["HELLO", "HELLO", "HELP", "HELP", "HELP", "YES", "YES", "NO"],
     "environment": ["indoor", "outdoor", "outdoor", "outdoor",
                     "indoor", "indoor", "indoor", "outdoor"],
     "quality": [5, 4, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5, 5]
 })
# Task 1 — Sign distribution
 df["sign"].value_counts().plot(kind="bar")
 plt.xlabel("Sign")
 plt.ylabel("Number of Videos")
 plt.title("Distribution of Videos per Sign")
 plt.show()
# Task 2 — Quality distribution
 df["quality"].plot(kind="hist", bins=5, edgecolor="black")
 plt.xlabel("Quality Score")
 plt.ylabel("Frequency")
 plt.title("Distribution of Video Quality Scores")
 plt.show()
# Task 3 — Average quality by sign
 df.groupby("sign")["quality"].mean().plot(kind="bar")
 plt.xlabel("Sign")
 plt.ylabel("Average Quality Score")
 plt.title("Average Video Quality Score by Sign")
 plt.show()
Task 4 — Research Observation
  * Observation 1: The sign "HELP" is the most frequent category in the dataset with 3 videos, while "NO" is the least frequent with only 1 video.
  * Observation 2: Video quality scores span from 2 to 5, with high-quality videos (scores 4 and 5) accounting for the majority of the dataset.
  * Observation 3: "HELP" has the lowest average quality score (3.0) among all signs, whereas "NO" achieves the highest average quality score (5.0).


